  "correct": "A",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Keep the product factored",
      "body": "<p>A product is nonnegative when its factors have the same sign, or when either factor is zero. Find the zeros of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "A sign chart uses the zeros of every factor as its boundaries.",
      "pitfall": "Dividing by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> would need a sign case split and could lose its zero.",
      "pause": "Mark all three interior zero points on one number line.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l04",
          "reminder": "A sign chart uses the zeros of every factor as its boundaries."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Check signs between the boundaries",
      "body": "<p>The boundaries are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is negative between its two zeros; cosine is negative after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "The sign is constant between consecutive zero points of these continuous factors.",
      "pitfall": "A negative times a negative is also nonnegative.",
      "pause": "Identify intervals where both signs agree.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l16",
          "reminder": "The sign is constant between consecutive zero points of these continuous factors."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Retain the allowed zero endpoints",
      "body": "<p>Complete the sign chart for the two factors. Select intervals where the factors have the same sign or where a factor is zero, checking the domain endpoints separately.</p>",
      "recap": "A non-strict product inequality includes the factor zeros.",
      "pitfall": "An allowed domain endpoint need not satisfy the product inequality.",
      "pause": "State the union of intervals, including exactly the boundary points that satisfy the inequality.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l04",
          "reminder": "A non-strict product inequality includes the factor zeros."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Both factors are nonnegative on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Both are nonpositive on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. On each remaining interval their signs differ.</p><p>Including the zero endpoints gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>: <strong>A.</strong></p>",
